\begin_body

\begin_layout Itemize
The quantum harmonic oscillator is an important system in quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The time-independent Schrödinger equation may be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{d^{2}\psi}{dx^{2}}+\frac{1}{2}m\omega^{2}x^{2}\psi=E\psi,
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is the oscillator position
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In this system,
 we identify the Hamiltonian as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}+\frac{1}{2}m\omega^{2}x^{2},
\]

\end_inset

so that we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)=E_{n}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)\label{eq:qho-tise}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We may also write this using Dirac's bra-ket formalism:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}\ket{\psi_{n}}=E_{n}\ket{\psi_{n}}\label{eq:qho-tise-braket}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
It may be shown that the functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

–called eigenfunctions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

,
 each with its own energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset

– have energies given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{n}=\hbar\omega\left(n+\frac{1}{2}\right),\qquad n\geq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The eigenenergies are evenly spaced in energy
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}=\hbar\omega/2$
\end_inset

 is the ground state energy associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{0}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which is known as the ground state
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The ground state is the lowest allowed energy for the quantum harmonic oscillator
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{1}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{1}=3\hbar\omega/2$
\end_inset

 is called the first excited state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{2}=5\hbar\omega/2$
\end_inset

 is called the second excited state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The energy spacing,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar\omega$
\end_inset

 between states 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n\pm1}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is called the energy quantization
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This is a quantum of energy,
 also called a vibron
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In semiconductors,
 these quanta are called phonons
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
There is an infinte number of eigenfunctions and eigenenergies:
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\left\{ 0,1,2,\ldots\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The general quantum state of a quantum harmonic oscillator may be a superposition of eigenstates:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\psi\left(x\right)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}c_{n}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)\label{eq:arbitrary-QHO-state}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Here,
 the eigenfunctions 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \psi_{n}\left(x\right)\right\} $
\end_inset

 form an infinite basis for the quantum state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In practice,
 contributions to 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 from lower values of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 are most significant,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|c_{n}\right|$
\end_inset

 for large 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 becomes very small
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
In this expansion,
 we are using the energy eigenstates as a basis for an arbitrary 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We refer to this as the energy basis or the Fock basis
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In atomic units,
 we might write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H=\hbar\omega\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}+\frac{1}{2}\hat{1}\right)\rightarrow\frac{H}{\hbar\omega}=\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}+\frac{1}{2}\hat{1}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{x}=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega}}\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}+\hat{a}\right),
\]

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sqrt{\frac{2m\omega}{\hbar}}\hat{x}=\hat{a}^{\dagger}+\hat{a}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We can define
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{0}=\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{m\omega}}
\]

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{x}{x_{0}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}+\hat{a}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can define position and momentum operators,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{x}=x,\qquad\text{and}\qquad\hat{p}=i\hbar\frac{d}{dx}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we can write the Hamiltonian as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\frac{\hat{p}^{2}}{2m}+\frac{1}{2}m\omega\hat{x}^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We also may define creation and annihilation operators,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}^{\dagger}=\sqrt{\frac{m\omega}{2\hbar}}\left(\hat{x}+\frac{i}{m\omega}\hat{p}\right)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\hat{a}=\sqrt{\frac{m\omega}{2\hbar}}\left(\hat{x}-\frac{i}{m\omega}\hat{p}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We also can write the Hamiltonian in terms of these:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}\left(\hbar\omega+\frac{1}{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can write the position and momentum operators in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\hat{x} & =\sqrt{\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega}}\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}+\hat{a}\right),\label{eq:qho-position-operator}\\
\hat{p} & =\sqrt{\frac{\hbar m\omega}{2}}\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}-\hat{a}\right)\label{eq:qho-momentum-operator}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 transforms an eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{a}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)=\begin{cases}
\sqrt{n}\psi_{n-1}\left(x\right), & n>0\\
0, & n=0
\end{cases}\label{eq:lowering-action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 reduces the state's energy by one vibron.
 In other words,
 it annihilates one quanta of vibration,
 so we call this operator the annihilation operator
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 transforms an eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{a}^{\dagger}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)=\sqrt{n+1}\psi_{n+1}\left(x\right)\label{eq:raising-action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 increases the state's energy by one vibron.
 In other words,
 it creates one quanta of vibration,
 so we call this operator the creation operator
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Consider the action of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 on a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)=\sqrt{n}\hat{a}^{\dagger}\psi_{n-1}\left(x\right)=n\psi_{n}\left(x\right)\label{eq:number-operator-action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The action of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 is to extract 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 the number of quanta in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{n}\left(n\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 as the number operator,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{n}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{n}=\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}\label{eq:define-number-operator}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The integer 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is also called the occupation number,
 because it tells us how many quanta of vibration exist in a quantum state
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section
Computer Modeling of the QHO
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Energy Basis Representation for the QHO
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We start by truncating our infinite basis to a basis with 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 elements,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \psi_{0}\left(x\right),\psi_{1}\left(x\right),\ldots,\psi_{N-1}\left(x\right)\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 so we have an approximation to 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi\left(x\right)\simeq\sum_{n=0}^{N}c_{n}\psi_{n}\left(x\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Next,
 we assign elementary vectors to each eigenstate in our truncated basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{0}\left(x\right)\leftrightarrow\vec{e}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
0\\
\vdots
\end{array}\right];\quad\psi_{1}\left(x\right)\leftrightarrow\vec{e}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1\\
0\\
\vdots
\end{array}\right]\quad\cdots\quad\psi_{N-1}\left(x\right)\leftrightarrow\vec{e}_{N}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\vdots\\
0\\
0\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 we can write the matrix representation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 operator by finding the individual columns of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \psi_{0}\left(x\right),\psi_{1}\left(x\right),\ldots,\psi_{N-1}\left(x\right)\right\} \leftrightarrow\left\{ \vec{e}_{1},\vec{e}_{2},\ldots,\vec{e}_{N}\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The first column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis is the result of multiplying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 operator in the energy basis by the first elementary vector:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}\ket{0}=0\leftrightarrow\hat{a}\vec{e}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0\\
\vdots\\
0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The second column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis is the result of multiplying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 operator in the energy basis by the second elementary vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}\ket{1}=\ket{0}\leftrightarrow\hat{a}\vec{e}_{2}=\vec{e}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0\\
0\\
\vdots
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The third column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis is the result of multiplying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 operator in the energy basis by the third elementary vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}\ket{2}=\sqrt{2}\ket{1}\leftrightarrow\hat{a}\vec{e}_{3}=\sqrt{2}\vec{e}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\sqrt{2}\\
0\\
\vdots
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then,
 we can assemble all the columns to obtain the matrix representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}\leftrightarrow\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
0 & 1 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & 0 & \sqrt{2} & \cdots & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0 & \ddots & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \cdots & \sqrt{N-1}\\
0 & 0 & 0 & \cdots & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
All elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis are zero,
 except the following:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{n,n+1}=\sqrt{n}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can then easily find the matrix representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{a}^{\dagger}\leftrightarrow\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
0 & 0 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
1 & 0 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & \sqrt{2} & 0 & \ddots & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \cdots\\
0 & 0 & 0 & \sqrt{N-1} & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Next,
 we can the matrix representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hbar\omega\left(\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}+\frac{1}{2}\hat{I}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

