\series bold
Exercise 8.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Write Python code to form the raising and lowering operators of a quantum Harmonic oscillator with a basis truncated to 
\begin_inset Formula $N=6$
\end_inset

 states.
 Use 
\begin_inset Formula $m=6\;\text{amu}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f=300\;\text{inv. cm.}$
\end_inset

 Before we get too far,
 we must discuss units.
 Einstein's famous equation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E=mc^{2},
\]

\end_inset

tells us that the energy contained in the mass of a particle is proportional to its rest mass,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

,
 by the speed of light squared.
 One atomic mass unit (amu) is equivalent to 931 MeV.
 Additionally,
 a quantum relationship,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c=f\lambda,
\]

\end_inset

helps us understand frequency in inverse centimeters.
 This formula states that the product of a photon's frequency 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and its wavelength,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 the speed of light:
 
\begin_inset Formula $2.997\times10^{8}$
\end_inset

 m/s,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $2.997\times10^{10}$
\end_inset

 cm/s.
 We can rearrange this to be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\frac{c}{\lambda}=\frac{2.997\times10^{10}\;\text{cm/s}}{\lambda\;\text{cm}}.
\]

\end_inset

In quantum or relativistic sciences,
 it is sometimes customary to use atomic units,
 where we let physical constants such as 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

,
 etc.,
 all be unity.
 Under this convention,
 frequency takes units of inverse centimeters.
 We can simply take a frequency given in inverse centimeters and multiply by 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in Hertz:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{\text{Hz}}=cf_{\text{inv. cm.}}.
\]

\end_inset

Given 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 in Hz,
 we can then find angular frequency,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=2\pi f_{\text{Hz}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

