\begin_layout Itemize
We can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 is diagonal in the energy basis
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
You might recall from linear algebra that the eigenvalues of a diagonal matrix are its diagonal elements,
 and the elementary vectors are the associated eigenvalues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can immediately read the eigenenergies from the diagonal elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 in the energy basis
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The elementary basis vectors may not be incredibly revealing to us,
 because they represents the eigenstates in the energy basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 it is desirable to transform these states to the position basis
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
To do this,
 we can:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
form the position operator in the energy basis using Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:qho-position-operator"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
calculate the eigenvectors of the position operator in the energy basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
form the eigenvectors into a matrix
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This matrix transforms position-basis states into energy basis states
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the Hermitian adjoint of this matrix can be used to transform energy-basis states or operators to the position basis
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
